Security in Power BI

A comprehensive overview of Power BI's security layers for controlling data access, object visibility, and user permissions.

Row-Level Security (RLS)

Row-Level Security restricts data access at the row level based on user identity or role membership.

How it works:
• Define roles in Power BI Desktop using DAX filter expressions
• Assign users or groups to roles in the Power BI service
• Filters are applied automatically when users view reports—they only see rows permitted by their role

Types:
• Static RLS — Hardcoded filter values (e.g., [Region] = "West")
• Dynamic RLS — Uses USERPRINCIPALNAME() or USERNAME() to filter based on the logged-in user

Example DAX filter:
``dax
[SalesRepEmail] = USERPRINCIPALNAME()
`

Key considerations:
• RLS applies to Viewers, not Admins or Members with edit permissions
• Test roles using "View as Role" in Desktop before publishing
• Works with DirectQuery, Import, and composite models

Object-Level Security (OLS)

Object-Level Security hides specific tables or columns from users, preventing them from even seeing that the object exists.

Use cases:
• Hide sensitive columns (e.g., salary, SSN) from certain roles
• Restrict access to entire tables for specific user groups

Implementation:
• Configured via Tabular Editor or XMLA endpoints (not available in Power BI Desktop UI)
• Requires roles defined similarly to RLS
• Set None permission on tables/columns to hide them

Limitations:
• Requires Power BI Premium or PPU
• Users who lack access see an error if a visual references hidden objects

Workspace Roles

Workspaces are containers for dashboards, reports, datasets, and dataflows. Access is controlled through four built-in roles:

| Role | Permissions |
|------|-------------|
| Viewer | View and interact with content only |
| Contributor | Create, edit, and delete content; cannot manage permissions |
| Member | All Contributor permissions + share items and allow others to reshare |
| Admin | Full control including managing workspace settings and membership |

Best practices:
• Assign the least privilege necessary
• Use security groups rather than individual users for easier management
• Separate development and production workspaces

Dataset Permissions

Dataset permissions control who can connect to and build on a dataset, independent of workspace roles.

Permission types:
• Read — View reports built on the dataset
• Build — Create new reports using the dataset (enables "live connection")
• Reshare — Allow others to access the dataset
• Write — Modify the dataset (typically via XMLA endpoint)

Where to manage:
• Dataset settings → Manage permissions
• Can grant Build permission to users outside the workspace

Note: Users with Build permission can see all data in the dataset unless RLS is configured.

Data Protection

Power BI integrates with Microsoft Purview Information Protection (formerly MIP) to classify, label, and protect data.

Sensitivity labels:
• Apply labels like Confidential or Highly Confidential to reports, dashboards, datasets, and dataflows
• Labels persist when data is exported to Excel, PDF, or PowerPoint
• Inheritance: labels can flow from dataset → report → dashboard

Capabilities:
• Encryption — Prevent unauthorized access to exported files
• Watermarking — Visual indicators on sensitive content
• DLP policies — Block sharing or export of labeled content based on rules

Setup requirements:
• Microsoft 365 E5 or equivalent licensing
• Labels configured in the Microsoft Purview compliance portal
• Enabled in Power BI Admin portal under "Information protection"

